1
00:00:00,170 --> 00:00:03,200
I hope you had some fun playing around with some of the code blocks.

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:06,800
So let's continue our learning journey and see what else we can do.

3
00:00:06,920 --> 00:00:13,610
But before we move on and build some awesome games and apps and animations, we first need to talk about

4
00:00:13,610 --> 00:00:17,540
what all these groups of code blocks are and what their purpose is.

5
00:00:17,540 --> 00:00:23,840
Because if we can properly understand what each group does from the start, you will find it much easier

6
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:25,910
to create your own code.

7
00:00:26,490 --> 00:00:30,780
As we've seen, all the blocks in scratch are organized into different groups.

8
00:00:30,780 --> 00:00:36,600
As you can see, all these are blue, these are purple, these are pink, and so on.

9
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:42,810
Basically, each of these are grouped by what they do and they represent different aspects of programming.

10
00:00:42,810 --> 00:00:47,820
So let's go through each of the groups to understand their purpose, and also see some of the fun things

11
00:00:47,820 --> 00:00:48,780
that they can do.

12
00:00:49,850 --> 00:00:56,540
Virtually all these blue blocks are to do with motion and moving your sprite around the screen, and

13
00:00:56,540 --> 00:01:01,700
we've been playing quite a bit with this group of code, so I'm sure you have a pretty good idea of

14
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:03,170
what it's all about.

15
00:01:03,170 --> 00:01:06,710
But there is a bunch more you can do here using motion blocks.

16
00:01:06,710 --> 00:01:08,150
But moving on.

17
00:01:08,270 --> 00:01:10,070
Yeah, there's going to be funny.

18
00:01:10,280 --> 00:01:14,390
Uh, the next group is looks, and this is all about.

19
00:01:14,390 --> 00:01:16,490
Yeah, well, you guessed it looks.

20
00:01:16,490 --> 00:01:16,850
So.

21
00:01:16,850 --> 00:01:17,660
Let's have a look.

22
00:01:18,620 --> 00:01:24,680
We have also tried out some of the code blocks, like switching the costumes, saying different words,

23
00:01:24,680 --> 00:01:30,020
or even changing the color, but there's still a lot more we can do, like change the size and make

24
00:01:30,020 --> 00:01:34,310
the cat bigger, or make it hide or show and lots more.

25
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:40,280
But basically all you need to know is that the looks group is all to do with looks, and we will often

26
00:01:40,280 --> 00:01:46,820
use this when we want to create fancy graphics and animations, which you will see later on.

27
00:01:47,530 --> 00:01:48,310
Moving down.

28
00:01:48,310 --> 00:01:52,390
We have sounds, and I'm sure you can guess what this is about.

29
00:01:52,450 --> 00:01:55,720
Here we can have our sprites make specific sounds.

30
00:01:55,720 --> 00:01:59,920
So with our cat, we can have it make this meow sound.

31
00:02:00,220 --> 00:02:02,350
And we can also change the pitch, as you can see.

32
00:02:03,110 --> 00:02:05,720
And yes, it does start to get a bit crazy.

33
00:02:06,050 --> 00:02:06,620
Okay.

34
00:02:06,620 --> 00:02:13,520
Moving down, our next group is called events, and you might not be too sure what this group is about.

35
00:02:13,550 --> 00:02:19,700
However, this is very important group of code blocks because it allows us to automatically play our

36
00:02:19,700 --> 00:02:23,090
scripts when a certain event happens.

37
00:02:23,420 --> 00:02:29,660
So far we have used the when flag clicked event, which basically starts our code scripts when this

38
00:02:29,660 --> 00:02:34,790
green flag is clicked, and you'll see how useful this is when we have lots of sprites.

39
00:02:34,790 --> 00:02:38,420
But as you can see, there are other events like when space clicked.

40
00:02:38,420 --> 00:02:44,300
This will only start the script when the spacebar is clicked, or this event starts, the script when

41
00:02:44,300 --> 00:02:48,680
the sprite is clicked, or when I receive a message.

42
00:02:48,830 --> 00:02:51,200
These events all do the same thing.

43
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:56,630
Instead of waiting for us to click the green flag to play our code, they will wait for something else

44
00:02:56,630 --> 00:02:57,350
to happen.

45
00:02:57,350 --> 00:03:00,710
And this allows us to control when code should start.

46
00:03:00,740 --> 00:03:06,500
Now you will really get to know how this all works when we make some more advanced applications later,

47
00:03:06,500 --> 00:03:11,270
but with that, we can move to our next group of code, which is control.

48
00:03:11,820 --> 00:03:17,970
All the blocks in here are used to control our code, which basically means they help us manage the

49
00:03:17,970 --> 00:03:19,860
flow and structure of our scripts.

50
00:03:19,860 --> 00:03:25,290
Kind of like how traffic lights help direct cars at busy intersections.

51
00:03:25,650 --> 00:03:31,320
For example, we have blocks for creating loops, which let us repeat sections of code a specific number

52
00:03:31,320 --> 00:03:32,970
of times or even forever.

53
00:03:33,030 --> 00:03:38,100
Just like we did earlier to get our cat to walk to the end of the screen or constantly follow our mouse

54
00:03:38,100 --> 00:03:38,760
pointer.

55
00:03:39,330 --> 00:03:45,390
We also have conditional statements like if, then and if, then else blocks which allow sprites to

56
00:03:45,390 --> 00:03:51,960
make decisions based on certain conditions being met, similar to how you might choose to wear a coat

57
00:03:51,960 --> 00:03:55,890
if it's raining or wear sunglasses if it's sunny outside.

58
00:03:56,800 --> 00:03:59,830
There's also this block here which weights.

59
00:04:00,220 --> 00:04:07,360
Moving this into our script, you will notice how now when I click the flag, it waits for one second

60
00:04:07,360 --> 00:04:08,890
before it plays the script.

61
00:04:09,370 --> 00:04:13,900
This is also an important thing to note, so pay close attention.

62
00:04:14,430 --> 00:04:16,410
When we run one of our scripts, like this one.

63
00:04:16,410 --> 00:04:21,870
For example, the code runs in a top down order, so we can look at it logically.

64
00:04:21,870 --> 00:04:27,780
When we click the flag, it will first wait for one second, and this is the first block of code.

65
00:04:27,780 --> 00:04:33,120
Then it will change color by 25 and then point towards the mouse pointer.

66
00:04:33,120 --> 00:04:36,150
And of course this will forever loop this code here.

67
00:04:36,760 --> 00:04:41,050
But if you play the script, it does seem like it is.

68
00:04:41,050 --> 00:04:46,030
Both these code blocks are running at the same time, and this is because computers are so fast that

69
00:04:46,030 --> 00:04:48,970
you can't even notice this is happening in order.

70
00:04:49,360 --> 00:04:51,970
But let's add some white blocks to see it in action.

71
00:04:52,660 --> 00:04:59,770
So dragging in our wait one second here and another here before I run the code, can you guess what

72
00:04:59,770 --> 00:05:00,610
it's going to do?

73
00:05:00,640 --> 00:05:01,420
Think?

74
00:05:01,660 --> 00:05:02,500
Guess.

75
00:05:03,100 --> 00:05:04,930
What will the weights do?

76
00:05:06,150 --> 00:05:08,790
We click the flag, it waits for one second.

77
00:05:08,790 --> 00:05:14,460
It changes color, waits for another second, points towards the mouse, waits for another second.

78
00:05:14,460 --> 00:05:17,310
Then it goes back to changing the color and so on.

79
00:05:17,310 --> 00:05:23,370
So playing this program, you can see how it does exactly what we predicted and how it happens in the

80
00:05:23,370 --> 00:05:25,080
order we specified.

81
00:05:25,560 --> 00:05:29,970
And as we move on, you'll see how the order in which we place our code is so important.

82
00:05:30,240 --> 00:05:36,300
But on that topic, these control blocks give us the power to make our project smarter and more interactive

83
00:05:36,300 --> 00:05:38,850
by responding to different situations as they happen.

84
00:05:38,850 --> 00:05:42,090
And there's still a bunch more, but you will get the idea.

85
00:05:42,090 --> 00:05:42,750
For now.

86
00:05:42,750 --> 00:05:47,790
Moving down to the next group, we have sensing and these blocks are pretty cool.

87
00:05:47,790 --> 00:05:54,240
Sensing blocks let your sprites detect things like touching another sprite, a mouse movement, or even

88
00:05:54,240 --> 00:05:57,030
listening for what a user types.

89
00:05:57,030 --> 00:06:03,240
These blocks help your projects react to their environment, and this is very handy when we want to

90
00:06:03,240 --> 00:06:08,280
build interactive games and we'll play with some of these later on when we make some fun games.

91
00:06:08,870 --> 00:06:09,950
Moving down again.

92
00:06:09,950 --> 00:06:13,730
We have operators and this is all to do with math.

93
00:06:13,730 --> 00:06:20,330
And yeah, yeah, math can be boring sometimes, but I will tell you that when we are making games,

94
00:06:20,330 --> 00:06:25,430
we need some math to make it work and it becomes a whole lot of fun.

95
00:06:26,030 --> 00:06:31,190
Operators are kind of like the tools we use to build and solve puzzles in our project.

96
00:06:31,850 --> 00:06:37,610
They help us do math and make comparisons, just like we do when we are figuring things out in everyday

97
00:06:37,610 --> 00:06:38,090
life.

98
00:06:38,090 --> 00:06:43,790
For example, we can add, subtract, multiply, or divide numbers together.

99
00:06:43,790 --> 00:06:49,190
Find the remainder when one number is divided by another, or even pick a random number.

100
00:06:50,030 --> 00:06:51,140
These operators.

101
00:06:51,140 --> 00:06:56,540
Also, let us compare values to see if they're equal greater than or less than one another.

102
00:06:56,570 --> 00:07:01,910
It's like when you're playing a card game and you need to figure out who has the highest card.

103
00:07:01,940 --> 00:07:06,050
We use comparisons to decide who wins and not just numbers.

104
00:07:06,080 --> 00:07:12,440
We can even combine text, also known as strings, to create sentences or make our sprites say pretty

105
00:07:12,440 --> 00:07:13,280
funny things.

106
00:07:14,010 --> 00:07:20,310
So operators give us a whole bunch of cool tools to work with when we're creating our games and projects

107
00:07:20,310 --> 00:07:25,500
in scratch, and they'll help us make our projects more dynamic and interactive.

108
00:07:26,160 --> 00:07:28,920
And finally we have variables.

109
00:07:29,220 --> 00:07:34,620
Variables are like little storage boxes for information that we can use in our projects.

110
00:07:34,830 --> 00:07:38,400
Imagine you're playing a game and and you have a score.

111
00:07:38,550 --> 00:07:40,770
Where would you keep track of that score?

112
00:07:41,040 --> 00:07:43,530
You got it in a variable.

113
00:07:44,170 --> 00:07:44,980
In scratch.

114
00:07:44,980 --> 00:07:51,550
We can create our very own variables to store and keep track of different values like scores, lives,

115
00:07:51,550 --> 00:07:53,080
or even a player's name.

116
00:07:53,820 --> 00:07:59,220
We can change these values by adding, subtracting, setting, or even showing and hiding the variable

117
00:07:59,220 --> 00:08:00,090
on the screen.

118
00:08:00,630 --> 00:08:05,190
Just like in the real world, we use notes to remember important information that we might need later

119
00:08:05,190 --> 00:08:05,700
on.

120
00:08:05,700 --> 00:08:09,420
In scratch and programming, we use variables.

121
00:08:09,420 --> 00:08:15,510
For example, let's say you're making a game where a character collects coins each time they collect

122
00:08:15,510 --> 00:08:16,110
a coin.

123
00:08:16,110 --> 00:08:18,480
You'd want to increase this score by one.

124
00:08:18,600 --> 00:08:23,610
You could use a variable to store the player's score, and then add one to it every time they pick up

125
00:08:23,610 --> 00:08:24,270
a coin.

126
00:08:24,770 --> 00:08:30,470
Variables are super helpful in making our games and projects more engaging, interactive, and fun.

127
00:08:30,470 --> 00:08:35,690
Plus, they help us keep track of important information as our projects get more complex.

128
